    },
    {
      "id": "2RGQSly0GG1OyB0SLazT_",
      "type": "text",
      "x": 205,
      "y": 239,
      "width": 621,
      "height": 2400,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "backgroundColor": "transparent",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "a2",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 973474934,
      "version": 984,
      "versionNonce": 984706913,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": [],
      "updated": 1786345519461,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "When parent class has paramaterized constructor then java\nrequires two things\n\n1. child must contain a constructor .\n\n2. from child class constructor it become compulsorily to call\nthe parent class \ncontructor using the \"super\" keyword .\n\n3. A Child constructor can directly call only one Parent\n   constructor because super() can be used only once and\n   must be the first statement.\n\n4. If we want multiple Parent constructors to execute,\n   we must use constructor chaining within the Parent class\n   using this().\n\n5. Every constructor has non paramterized super() by default .\n\n\n\nConstructor Chaining with Type Conversion\n==========================================\n\n1. Java supports automatic widening conversion, so an int\n   value can be assigned to a double.\n\n   Example:\n   int x = 10;\n   double d = x;       // ✅\n\n\n2. The same conversion is also possible while selecting\n   an overloaded constructor.\n\n   Example:\n\n   Vehicle2(int x) {\n       this((double)x);\n   }\n\n   Vehicle2(double d) {\n       // ...\n   }\n\n\n3. Here, this((double)x) is not required because Java cannot\n   convert int to double.\n\n   Java CAN automatically convert int to double.\n\n\n4. The cast (double) is used to specifically select the\n   double-parameterized constructor.\n\n   this((double)x)\n          ↓\n   Vehicle2(double)\n\n\n5. If we write:\n\n   this(x);\n\n   inside Vehicle2(int), Java will try to call the same\n   Vehicle2(int) constructor again.\n\n   Vehicle2(int)\n        ↓\n      this(x)\n        ↓\n   Vehicle2(int)\n        ↓\n      this(x)\n        ↓\n      ...\n\n   This causes recursive constructor invocation and gives\n   a compile-time error.\n\n\n6. Therefore, we use:\n\n   this((double)x);\n\n   to force constructor chaining from the int constructor\n   to the double constructor.\n\n\nImportant:\n-----------\n\nint → double is automatically possible in Java.\n\nThe (double) cast here is mainly used to select the\ndouble constructor, not because conversion is compulsory.",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "When parent class has paramaterized constructor then java requires two things\n\n1. child must contain a constructor .\n\n2. from child class constructor it become compulsorily to call the parent class \ncontructor using the \"super\" keyword .\n\n3. A Child constructor can directly call only one Parent\n   constructor because super() can be used only once and\n   must be the first statement.\n\n4. If we want multiple Parent constructors to execute,\n   we must use constructor chaining within the Parent class\n   using this().\n\n5. Every constructor has non paramterized super() by default .\n\n\n\nConstructor Chaining with Type Conversion\n==========================================\n\n1. Java supports automatic widening conversion, so an int\n   value can be assigned to a double.\n\n   Example:\n   int x = 10;\n   double d = x;       // ✅\n\n\n2. The same conversion is also possible while selecting\n   an overloaded constructor.\n\n   Example:\n\n   Vehicle2(int x) {\n       this((double)x);\n   }\n\n   Vehicle2(double d) {\n       // ...\n   }\n\n\n3. Here, this((double)x) is not required because Java cannot\n   convert int to double.\n\n   Java CAN automatically convert int to double.\n\n\n4. The cast (double) is used to specifically select the\n   double-parameterized constructor.\n\n   this((double)x)\n          ↓\n   Vehicle2(double)\n\n\n5. If we write:\n\n   this(x);\n\n   inside Vehicle2(int), Java will try to call the same\n   Vehicle2(int) constructor again.\n\n   Vehicle2(int)\n        ↓\n      this(x)\n        ↓\n   Vehicle2(int)\n        ↓\n      this(x)\n        ↓\n      ...\n\n   This causes recursive constructor invocation and gives\n   a compile-time error.\n\n\n6. Therefore, we use:\n\n   this((double)x);\n\n   to force constructor chaining from the int constructor\n   to the double constructor.\n\n\nImportant:\n-----------\n\nint → double is automatically possible in Java.\n\nThe (double) cast here is mainly used to select the\ndouble constructor, not because conversion is compulsory.",
