      "locked": false,
      "text": "# Static Method and Instance Method in Inheritance\n\n1. A static method belongs to the class, whereas an instance method belongs to an object.\n\n2. Static and instance methods use different method dispatch mechanisms.\n\n   Static method → resolved at compile time based on the reference/class.\n\n   Instance method → resolved at runtime based on the actual object.\n\n3. Therefore, a child class cannot declare an instance method with the same signature as an inherited static method.\n\n```java\nclass Parent {\n    static void show() {\n    }\n}\n\nclass Child extends Parent {\n    void show() {     // ❌ Compile-time error\n    }\n}\n```\n\n4. Similarly, a child class cannot declare a static method with the same signature as an inherited instance method.\n\n5. The following combinations are allowed:\n\n   Parent static → Child static      ✅ Method Hiding\n\n   Parent instance → Child instance    ✅ Method Overriding\n\n6. The following combinations are not allowed:\n\n   Parent static → Child instance    ❌\n\n   Parent instance → Child static    ❌\n\n### Key Point\n\nA method cannot change from static to instance or from instance to static in a subclass when the method signature is the same.\n\nReason: Static and instance methods have different dispatch rules, and Java does not allow the same inherited method signature to use both mechanisms.\n",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "# Static Method and Instance Method in Inheritance\n\n1. A static method belongs to the class, whereas an instance method belongs to an object.\n\n2. Static and instance methods use different method dispatch mechanisms.\n\n   Static method → resolved at compile time based on the reference/class.\n\n   Instance method → resolved at runtime based on the actual object.\n\n3. Therefore, a child class cannot declare an instance method with the same signature as an inherited static method.\n\n```java\nclass Parent {\n    static void show() {\n    }\n}\n\nclass Child extends Parent {\n    void show() {     // ❌ Compile-time error\n    }\n}\n```\n\n4. Similarly, a child class cannot declare a static method with the same signature as an inherited instance method.\n\n5. The following combinations are allowed:\n\n   Parent static → Child static      ✅ Method Hiding\n\n   Parent instance → Child instance    ✅ Method Overriding\n\n6. The following combinations are not allowed:\n\n   Parent static → Child instance    ❌\n\n   Parent instance → Child static    ❌\n\n### Key Point\n\nA method cannot change from static to instance or from instance to static in a subclass when the method signature is the same.\n\nReason: Static and instance methods have different dispatch rules, and Java does not allow the same inherited method signature to use both mechanisms.\n",
      "autoResize": true,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
    {
      "id": "cbECbNqDuPs45rwdGjUD3",
      "type": "text",
      "x": 132,
