      "boundElements": null,
      "updated": 1786684363628,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "# Method Overloading vs Method Overriding\n\n## Method Overloading\n\n1. Method overloading means having **multiple methods with the same name but different parameter lists**.\n\n2. It can occur **within the same class** and can also occur in **inheritance**.\n\n3. The parameter list must be different in at least one of the following:\n\n   * Number of parameters\n   * Type of parameters\n   * Order of parameters\n\n4. Changing only the return type is **not sufficient** for method overloading.\n\n5. Method overloading is an example of **compile-time polymorphism**.\n\n```java\nclass Test {\n    void show(int x) {\n    }\n\n    void show(double x) {\n    }\n\n    void show(int x, int y) {\n    }\n}\n```\n\n---\n\n## Method Overriding\n\n1. Method overriding occurs when a **child class provides its own implementation of a method already defined in the parent class**.\n\n2. It is possible **only in inheritance**.\n\n3. The method name and parameter list must be the **same**.\n\n4. The return type must be the same or **covariant**.\n\n5. Method overriding is an example of **runtime polymorphism**.\n\n```java\nclass Parent {\n    void show() {\n        System.out.println(\"Parent\");\n    }\n}\n\nclass Child extends Parent {\n    @Override\n    void show() {\n        System.out.println(\"Child\");\n    }\n}\n```\n\n---\n\n## Main Difference\n\n```text\nOverloading:\nSame method name + Different parameter list\n→ Inheritance not required\n→ Compile-time polymorphism\n\nOverriding:\nSame method name + Same parameter list\n→ Inheritance required\n→ Runtime polymorphism\n```\n\n## Important Rules\n\n```text\nOverloading:\nName same       → Yes\nArguments same  → No\nInheritance     → Not required\nReturn type     → Cannot be the only difference\n\nOverriding:\nName same       → Yes\nArguments same  → Yes\nInheritance     → Required\nImplementation  → Child provides new implementation\n```\n\n### Easy Way to Remember\n\n**Overloading → Same name, different arguments**\n\n**Overriding → Same method, child changes implementation**\n",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "# Method Overloading vs Method Overriding\n\n## Method Overloading\n\n1. Method overloading means having **multiple methods with the same name but different parameter lists**.\n\n2. It can occur **within the same class** and can also occur in **inheritance**.\n\n3. The parameter list must be different in at least one of the following:\n\n   * Number of parameters\n   * Type of parameters\n   * Order of parameters\n\n4. Changing only the return type is **not sufficient** for method overloading.\n\n5. Method overloading is an example of **compile-time polymorphism**.\n\n```java\nclass Test {\n    void show(int x) {\n    }\n\n    void show(double x) {\n    }\n\n    void show(int x, int y) {\n    }\n}\n```\n\n---\n\n## Method Overriding\n\n1. Method overriding occurs when a **child class provides its own implementation of a method already defined in the parent class**.\n\n2. It is possible **only in inheritance**.\n\n3. The method name and parameter list must be the **same**.\n\n4. The return type must be the same or **covariant**.\n\n5. Method overriding is an example of **runtime polymorphism**.\n\n```java\nclass Parent {\n    void show() {\n        System.out.println(\"Parent\");\n    }\n}\n\nclass Child extends Parent {\n    @Override\n    void show() {\n        System.out.println(\"Child\");\n    }\n}\n```\n\n---\n\n## Main Difference\n\n```text\nOverloading:\nSame method name + Different parameter list\n→ Inheritance not required\n→ Compile-time polymorphism\n\nOverriding:\nSame method name + Same parameter list\n→ Inheritance required\n→ Runtime polymorphism\n```\n\n## Important Rules\n\n```text\nOverloading:\nName same       → Yes\nArguments same  → No\nInheritance     → Not required\nReturn type     → Cannot be the only difference\n\nOverriding:\nName same       → Yes\nArguments same  → Yes\nInheritance     → Required\nImplementation  → Child provides new implementation\n```\n\n### Easy Way to Remember\n\n**Overloading → Same name, different arguments**\n\n**Overriding → Same method, child changes implementation**\n",
      "autoResize": true,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
