      "text": "# Method Hiding\n\n1. Method hiding occurs when a **child class defines a static method with the same signature as a static method in the parent class**.\n\n2. Method hiding is applicable only to **static methods**.\n\n3. Static methods are **not overridden** because they belong to the class, not to objects.\n\n4. In method hiding, the method to be executed is determined by the **reference/class type**, not by the actual object.\n\n```java\nclass Parent {\n    static void show() {\n        System.out.println(\"Parent\");\n    }\n}\n\nclass Child extends Parent {\n    static void show() {\n        System.out.println(\"Child\");\n    }\n}\n```\n\n5. Example:\n\n```java\nParent p = new Child();\n\np.show();       // Parent\n```\n\nHere, `Parent.show()` executes because `show()` is static and the reference type is `Parent`.\n\n```java\nChild c = new Child();\n\nc.show();       // Child\n```\n\nHere, `Child.show()` executes because the reference/class type is `Child`.\n\n## Method Hiding vs Method Overriding\n\n### Method Hiding\n\n* Parent method → `static`\n* Child method → `static`\n* Same method signature\n* Inheritance required\n* Compile-time binding\n* Reference/class type determines the method\n\n### Method Overriding\n\n* Parent method → instance\n* Child method → instance\n* Same method signature\n* Inheritance required\n* Runtime binding\n* Actual object determines the method\n\n### Key Point\n\n**Static methods are hidden, not overridden.**\n\n**Method Hiding = Parent static method + Child static method with the same signature.**\n",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "# Method Hiding\n\n1. Method hiding occurs when a **child class defines a static method with the same signature as a static method in the parent class**.\n\n2. Method hiding is applicable only to **static methods**.\n\n3. Static methods are **not overridden** because they belong to the class, not to objects.\n\n4. In method hiding, the method to be executed is determined by the **reference/class type**, not by the actual object.\n\n```java\nclass Parent {\n    static void show() {\n        System.out.println(\"Parent\");\n    }\n}\n\nclass Child extends Parent {\n    static void show() {\n        System.out.println(\"Child\");\n    }\n}\n```\n\n5. Example:\n\n```java\nParent p = new Child();\n\np.show();       // Parent\n```\n\nHere, `Parent.show()` executes because `show()` is static and the reference type is `Parent`.\n\n```java\nChild c = new Child();\n\nc.show();       // Child\n```\n\nHere, `Child.show()` executes because the reference/class type is `Child`.\n\n## Method Hiding vs Method Overriding\n\n### Method Hiding\n\n* Parent method → `static`\n* Child method → `static`\n* Same method signature\n* Inheritance required\n* Compile-time binding\n* Reference/class type determines the method\n\n### Method Overriding\n\n* Parent method → instance\n* Child method → instance\n* Same method signature\n* Inheritance required\n* Runtime binding\n* Actual object determines the method\n\n### Key Point\n\n**Static methods are hidden, not overridden.**\n\n**Method Hiding = Parent static method + Child static method with the same signature.**\n",
      "autoResize": true,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    }
